                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has quite a bit of air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Coming in through the outside air supply ductwork (with preheat from boiler)



** Above the false ceiling area in the hallway



** Coming in through the crawlspace (by the bathtub access panel, through heat ductwork, holes in floor). The crawlspace walls must have some holes through them that need to be sealed.



** The older windows throughout the house



** The current 6" chimney for the boiler. The combustion air pipe was blocked during my visit



Savings: If you proceed with a Deep Home Energy Retrofit of your house, I estimate you can achieve about a 30% reduction in air leakage. This is estimated to save $ 573 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.0 GJ, and qualify for a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 R20 with an additional 1.5" (R7.5) of extruded polystyrene foam board insulation on the exterior. You mentioned that you are planning to replace the siding on the house and would like to add some additional exterior insulation - enough for you to participate in the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).  I have modeled adding R10 (2" of extruded polystyrene rigid insulation), bringing the total R-value up to R37.5.



Savings: By adding another R10 to the exterior of the main floor walls, you could potentially save $ 272 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



As discussed, if you were not already planning an exterior siding upgrade, the cost/benefit of increasing the wall insulation from R27.5 to R37.5 is hard to justify. However, if you are already planning an exterior siding upgrade anyway, the $3,000 rebate will go a long way to covering the cost of the additional R10. Perhaps of greater importance to you is that, if you add the R10 to the walls, this will open up other rebates through DHERP that you would not otherwise qualify for:

- Up to $3,000 for upgrading up to 12 windows to ones that meet the eligibility criteria

- Up to $1,000 for reducing air leakage

- The boiler upgrade rebate will be doubled from $750 to $1,500

- An estimated $2,000 Bonus rebate if you reduce total energy use by 30%



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation>

The crawlspace below the main house has insulated walls and an insulated floor above. It does not appear this crawlspace is directly heated (ie: no dedicated boiler loop with its' own thermostat), however it does receive residual heat from the various boiler loops that travel through there to the zoned heating locations above.



The converted garage area on the right side has well insulated walls &amp; ceiling, however it appears the concrete slab in uninsulated, with a basic wood floor installed directly on top. If this is indeed the case, you should consider adding some insulation above the slab, under a new floor system above. I have modeled adding 3" (R15) of extruded polystyrene foam board insulation to the top of the concrete slab.



Savings: By adding R15 to the top of the concrete slab, this is estimated to save $ 402 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.7 GJ, There are currently no rebates for adding insulation to concrete slab foundations.</foundation>
                <windows>

You house has a total of 13 older wood windows, including the one that has been blocked off in the wall behind the kitchen stove. I recommend you upgrade these older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: 

By replacing these 13 windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 311 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.8 GJ, and qualify for $ 250 per window (12 windows maximum) in rebates through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

