                <AdditionalComments>

Wood Stove Supplemental Heat



I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of propane saved from a new high efficiency boiler &amp; the cost of wood in Hay River at the $100/cord "self-harvested" rate) if you were to use the wood stove for about 50% of the heating requirement for the main house. Getting that wood stove heat down the hall &amp; into the bedrooms will be a challenge. Perhaps you could set up some transfer fans in the false attic space that can draw the heat in &amp; then pipe it through the walls and into the bedrooms ?



The current wood stove has a Higher Heating Value (HHV) rated efficiency of only 63% and it does not have a dedicated outside combustion air pipe. I modeled an upgrade to a new EPA qualified woodstove that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and a rated HHV efficiency of at least 80%. Wood stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Rebates &amp; Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $ 2,432 per year in energy costs, in addition to the energy savings previously outlined for installing the 95% efficient propane combination boiler. Installing an eligible wood stove is eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs and fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM Pumps



Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



In Yellowknife, houses connected to the municipal water supply will have a stainless steel circulation pump that operates continuously throughout the year. This pump is also eligible for a $175 ECM pump rebate. Based on current electricity rates in Hay River, the estimated savings for upgrading this pump can be $130 - $230 per year !



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - a range hood (was broken during my visit)      

        - a bathroom fan

        - a clothes dryer



There is also an outside air supply ductwork system (with hydronic pre-heat of the incoming air). This system was not working at the time of my visit &amp; the previous occupants indicated it is never used as it just blows cold air ?? This is a "supply only" ventilation system that is considered unbalanced. You mentioned that you will be working with Taylor &amp; Company in Hay River to review the full heating &amp; ventilation design for this house.



You may want to consider installing an ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fan that seals well, uses little electricity &amp; operates at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood, bathroom fan and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 0.4 Pascals (very low as your house currently has a lot of air leakage, however this will change as you complete your renovations). As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your house currently has an oil boiler &amp; a wood stove that draws combustion air from inside the house &amp; both vent up a chimney. NOTE: there is a combustion air supply pipe for the boiler, however it was wrapped up &amp; sealed when I arrived.



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



